renameutils - the unflab-app fork
=================================

This is a maintained fork of renameutils, the file renaming utilities by
Oskar Liljeblad: qmv, qcp, imv, icp and deurlname. Upstream's last release
was 0.12.0, in April 2012.

   https://github.com/unflab-app/renameutils

The fork exists so that renameutils builds and works on current systems --
macOS in particular, where it is packaged for unflab (https://unflab.app/)
-- without the GNU tools it used to need at run time. It is a continuation,
not a rewrite: the programs, their options and their edit formats are the
ones described in the original README below.

Report bugs to the fork, not to the original author:

   https://github.com/unflab-app/renameutils/issues

What is different
-----------------

See NEWS for the full list. In short:

 * qmv and qcp no longer run `ls' to list files. They read directories
   themselves and order them the way GNU ls does, for every listing
   option they have always accepted. GNU Coreutils is not needed, and the
   --ls option is accepted but ignored.

 * Line editing works with GNU Readline or with libedit, which macOS
   provides. imv and icp pre-fill the old name under either, and use the
   system's mv and cp, GNU or BSD.

 * Names in UTF-8 appear as themselves in the editor, not as octal
   escapes. Control characters are still escaped, and are quoted in
   messages rather than written to the terminal.

 * Bug fixes: long listing options such as --sort were always rejected;
   deurlname could overwrite an existing file; a case-only rename failed
   on case-insensitive file systems; $PAGER was ignored; and others.

 * The French and Swedish translations in po/ are carried over, but have
   not been updated for the messages that changed, and are not tested.
   Translation stays enabled where gettext is available (configure
   --disable-nls turns it off).

 * qcmd and icmd are installed in libexec, with qmv, qcp, imv and icp
   linked to them from the bin directory. ipmiutil also has an icmd.

Requirements and building
-------------------------

To build: a C99 compiler, make, and GNU Readline or libedit.
`configure --with-readline=gnu' or `=libedit' chooses one; the default
is GNU Readline if it is found, otherwise libedit. Nothing else is needed
at run time beyond mv, cp and a text editor.

From a release tarball:

   ./configure && make && make check && make install

From a git checkout, which needs Autoconf 2.71, Automake 1.16, gettext
0.21 or later (autopoint, and msgfmt for `make dist'), and wget, first run

   ./bootstrap

which fetches the gnulib submodule and gnulib's own translations, and
generates configure. Releases are
tagged with plain version numbers (0.12.0, 0.13.0, ...); the tag `gnu'
marks the original 0.12.0 import, unchanged.

`make check' runs the test suite. The tests of imv, icp and qmv's
command mode drive a terminal through Python 3, and are skipped without
it.

Credits
-------

renameutils is the work of Oskar Liljeblad. Several fixes here come from
the Debian package maintained by Francois Marier, and from Chris
Hofstaedtler; see NEWS.


======================================================================
The original README for renameutils 0.12.0 follows, unchanged. Where it
differs from the notes above -- on requirements, the use of ls, or
where to report bugs -- the notes above are current.
======================================================================

renameutils - Introduction
==========================

The file renaming utilities (renameutils for short) are a set of programs
designed to make renaming of files faster and less cumbersome.

The file renaming utilities consists of five programs - qmv, qcp, imv, icp
and deurlname.

The qmv ("quick move") program allows file names to be edited in a text
editor. The names of all files in a directory are written to a text file,
which is then edited by the user. The text file is read and parsed, and the
changes are applied to the files.

The qcp ("quick cp") program works like qmv, but copies files instead of
moving them.

The imv ("interactive move") program, is trivial but useful when you are
too lazy to type (or even complete) the name of the file to rename twice.
It allows a file name to be edited in the terminal using the GNU Readline
library. icp copies files.

The deurlname program removes URL encoded characters (such as %20
representing space) from file names. Some programs such as w3m tend to keep
those characters encoded in saved files.

The file renaming utilities are written in C by Oskar Liljeblad, and is
free software licensed under terms of the GNU General Public License.

Note that these programs come with no warranty whatsoever. They should be
considered beta quality although I have carefully tested them and verified
the code. This means that I can not be responsible for any data loss caused
by the file renaming utilities. If you are afraid that that is going to
happen, run qmv in dummy mode first (see below).

